<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bay of Pigs invasion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bay of Pigs invasion was a failed landing in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> on 17–20 April 1961 by about 1,400 Cuban exiles trained and funded by the CIA, aiming to overthrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>. Without the hoped-for uprising or US air cover, they were defeated within three days and most were captured. The fiasco strengthened Castro, pushed Cuba closer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and preceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bay-of-pigs-invasion.jpg" alt="Bay of Pigs invasion" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rumlin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attack_near_Playa_Giron._April_19,_1961._-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By early 1960 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> had begun to consider ways of removing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a>, whose government had nationalised property owned by US citizens and strengthened its ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Eisenhower approved a plan by Richard Bissell that included training a paramilitary force of Cuban exiles, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> began an embargo of the island and severed diplomatic relations.</p><p>The exiles formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> 2506, the armed wing of the Cuban Democratic Revolutionary Front, and were trained by the CIA in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Kennedy, briefed as president on 28 January 1961, first considered a landing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>. The State Department rejected it because the airfield was too small for B-26 bombers, and on 4 April 1961 Kennedy approved the Bay of Pigs plan, known as Operation Zapata, which promised a long airfield and made denial of US involvement more plausible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 15 April 1961 eight CIA-supplied B-26 bombers attacked Cuban airfields. The main force, over 1,400 men in five infantry battalions and one paratrooper battalion, sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Nicaragua and landed on the night of 17 April at Playa Girón, Playa Larga and Caleta Buena. At Girón it overwhelmed a local revolutionary militia. The Cuban security services had known an invasion was coming, partly through indiscreet talk among the brigade's members.</p><p>José Ramón Fernández first led the Cuban counter-offensive, and Castro later took personal control. The brigade at Red Beach came under repeated counter-attacks, and its ammunition ran short. Kennedy decided to withhold further air support, although the plan had assumed US air and naval involvement, and the ships supplying the beachhead found it too dangerous to operate off the coast by day. The Cuban Revolutionary Armed Forces defeated the invaders within three days, and the brigade surrendered on 20 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Sixty-seven brigade members were killed in action; counting executions, deaths in custody and the four American airmen killed, the total was 106. Most of the surrendered men were publicly interrogated, imprisoned and tried. On 21 April Kennedy told a press conference that he was the responsible officer of the government. He later said that the CIA needed to be brought under control, and the failure led his administration to propose reorganising it.</p><p>The Taylor commission, a CIA inspector-general's report and the Cuban Revolutionary Council's split into rival factions followed. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> the victory made Castro more popular, and after the air attacks he declared the revolution Marxist–Leninist. The invasion widened the division between the two countries, pushed Cuba closer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and set the stage for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a> of 1962.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bay of Pigs Invasion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The United States imposed an embargo, and Castro declared the revolution socialist in April 1961, during the failed <b>Bay of Pigs invasion</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cuba">Republic of Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
